    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oruc-reis.jpg" alt="Oruç Reis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis">Oruç Reis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_465_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Theodor de Bry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> grew out of the struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Ottoman-backed corsairs for the coast of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, Spain seized a chain of North African ports, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir">Mers el-Kébir</a> in 1505, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1509 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> in 1510, and around 1510 it built a fort on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penon-of-algiers">Peñón</a>, an islet commanding the harbour of Algiers. In 1516 the town’s notables called on the corsair <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis">Oruç Barbarossa</a>, a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> who had been raiding Christian shipping from Tunisian and Algerian ports, to help them against the Spanish garrison. Oruç took Algiers, had its ruler Salim al-Tumi killed according to most accounts, and extended his control inland as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, where he was killed by Spanish forces in 1518. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a>, facing Spanish counter-attacks and local rivals, offered Algiers to the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> around 1519. In return he received the title of beylerbey, some 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and the right to recruit volunteers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In 1529 he captured the Peñón and joined the islets to the mainland with a mole, creating the sheltered harbour on which the corsair state depended. When he left to command the Ottoman fleet in 1533, Algiers was an established Ottoman province.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/algiers"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algiers.jpg" alt="Algiers" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_baie_d%27Alger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manini row, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> was a cosmopolitan port of perhaps 100,000 people in the early seventeenth century, though estimates vary widely. Its ruling class was a small Turkish-speaking military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> recruited mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, alongside Christian converts known as renegades, who supplied many of the leading corsair captains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouloughlis">Kouloughlis</a>, sons of Turkish soldiers and local women, were gradually excluded from high office and revolted unsuccessfully in 1633. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Jewish merchants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabyles">Kabyle</a> labourers and Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> townspeople made up the rest of the urban population, while the countryside remained largely tribal. The economy rested heavily on corsairing: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a></em> of the <em>reis</em>, the corporation of corsair captains, seized ships and cargoes and held Christian captives for ransom or labour. Captives were redeemed through religious orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinitarians">Trinitarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercedarians">Mercedarians</a>, who ransomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> in 1580 after five years in the city. Treaties obliged European states to pay tribute or send regular gifts in return for safe passage, and the regency also exported grain, wool, hides and wax, notably through Jewish trading houses such as the Bacri and Busnach in the late eighteenth century. The Ottoman elite followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of Islamic law, the majority of the population the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods and religious foundations carried great weight in the countryside.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto.jpg" alt="Battle of Lepanto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laureys_a_Castro_-_The_Battle_of_Lepanto.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laureys a Castro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regency’s power rested on two corps: the <em>ojaq</em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, which garrisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and the provincial towns and collected taxes with armed columns, and the corsair fleet, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and later sailing ships raided from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> to the Atlantic. In 1541 an expedition led by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in person was wrecked by a storm before Algiers, a defeat that secured the city for almost three centuries. Algerian forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1555 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salah-rais">Salah Rais</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanid</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in the 1550s and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir">Mers el-Kébir</a> in 1563 without success. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Uluj Ali</a>, a Calabrian convert, became beylerbey in 1568, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1569 and commanded the Ottoman left wing at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571. Corsairs from Algiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> raided Iceland in 1627, and in 1631 a raid led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-janszoon">Jan Janszoon</a>, known as Murat Reis, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltimore-county-cork">Baltimore</a> in Ireland. European navies retaliated repeatedly: the French admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-duquesne">Abraham Duquesne</a> bombarded the city in 1682 and 1683, and a large Spanish landing under Alejandro O’Reilly was repulsed in 1775. The regency captured Oran in 1708, lost it to Spain in 1732 and recovered it in 1792, and its armies imposed tribute on Tunis after taking that city in 1756.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Hayreddin Barbarossa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hayreddin_-_Algeria_Fine_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AsiaticLion7, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regency’s constitution changed several times. Until 1587 it was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylerbey">beylerbeys</a>, many of them leading corsairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin</a>’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-son-of-hayreddin">Hasan Pasha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Uluj Ali</a>. After Uluj Ali’s death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> replaced them with pashas appointed for three-year terms, whose authority was steadily eroded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> council, the <em>divan</em>. In 1659 the Janissaries took power and entrusted the government to their own aghas, each of whom was killed in office, and in 1671 the corsair captains imposed a dey, in principle elected by the <em>ojaq</em>. From about 1711 the dey also held the title of pasha, ending the sultan’s appointed governors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> conducted its own wars and treaties while recognising Ottoman suzerainty and sending gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. Deys ruled with a small council of ministers, including the <em>khaznaji</em> in charge of the treasury and the agha of the army; many were murdered by soldiers, though some reigned for decades. Outside the capital three beys governed the provinces of Constantine in the east, Titteri in the centre and the West, ruled first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazouna">Mazouna</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mascara-algeria">Mascara</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a>, while the Dar al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> around Algiers was administered directly. Allied <em>makhzen</em> tribes helped collect taxes, and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> remained largely autonomous.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jamaa-el-djedid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamaa-el-djedid.jpg" alt="Jamaa el Djedid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jamaa-el-djedid">Jamaa el Djedid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alger-Place-des-Martyrs-Casbah_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ludovic Courtès, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For three centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> was the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> states and a frontier bastion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1541 and its campaigns against Spanish outposts halted Iberian expansion in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, while its ships reinforced the Ottoman navy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Lepanto</a> and in other campaigns. The corsair war shaped European life far beyond the coast: many thousands of captives passed through the city’s prison-houses, or <em>bagnios</em>, and the ransoming of captives became a major charitable cause in Catholic and Protestant countries alike. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Cervantes</a> drew on his captivity in <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a></em> and in plays set in Algiers, and captivity narratives became a popular genre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> signed a treaty with Algiers in 1795 that paid tribute for the release of captive sailors, and the Barbary crises helped persuade Congress to build a standing navy. Within the Maghreb the regency gave the territory around Algiers a durable political centre and frontiers broadly similar to those of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> today; the country’s name itself derives from the city of al-Jaza’ir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casbah-of-algiers">Casbah of Algiers</a>, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaa-el-djedid">Jamaa el Djedid</a> mosque of 1660 survive from the period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regency’s strength ebbed from the late seventeenth century. Peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> narrowed the range of permitted prey, recruitment of Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> declined, and plague repeatedly struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, whose population shrank to perhaps 30,000 by 1830. The French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> briefly revived corsairing and grain exports, but after 1815 the European powers turned against the system. In 1815 an American squadron under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-decatur">Stephen Decatur</a> forced a treaty ending American tribute payments, and in August 1816 an Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-pellew-1st-viscount-exmouth">Lord Exmouth</a> bombarded Algiers and obtained the release of more than a thousand Christian captives. A dispute over debts for grain supplied to France in the 1790s by the Bacri and Busnach houses poisoned relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and in April 1827 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-dey">Hussein Dey</a> struck the French consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-deval">Pierre Deval</a> with a fly-whisk during an audience. France imposed a naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and, seeking prestige for the government of Charles X, landed an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidi-fredj">Sidi Ferruch</a> in June 1830. Algiers capitulated on 5 July 1830 and Hussein Dey went into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-bey-of-constantine">Ahmed Bey of Constantine</a> held out in the east until the French took his capital in 1837, but the regency had ended and its territory became French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> turned a port threatened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsair state and an Ottoman bastion in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> for over three centuries, shaping European captivity, diplomacy and naval history and giving the territory of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> its first lasting political centre before the French conquest of 1830.</em></p>

